00:07:17.704 --> 00:07:19.078
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Yeah, so we have

00:07:19.078 --> 00:07:21.317
a bit of an interesting
history here actually.

00:07:21.317 --> 00:07:23.400
Node.js started happening
around, I want to say it

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.010
was 2009 when it
started getting built.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:29.810
And it's built on top of V8,
the JavaScript execution engine

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:31.430
that's part of Google Chrome.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.440
So we spent all of these years
building up all this expertise

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.500
on optimizing JavaScript,
making it run as fast as humanly

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.200
possible in web browsers,
and then a group of folks

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.880
over at Join started building
and making it fast for running

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.414
on the server side as well.

00:07:45.414 --> 00:07:47.580
And for a couple of years,
you know, here at Google,

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.680
we have a set of
languages that we focus on

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.080
and JavaScript on
the server side

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.060
traditionally hasn't
been one of them.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.470
And so that effort kind
of went on in parallel.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:58.300
And really, I think over the
last few years, especially

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.870
this year, as you
Node.js has gotten closer

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:03.007
to being more viable for
enterprise application

00:08:03.007 --> 00:08:05.590
development, we're starting to
get a little bit more involved.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.272
We sort of sat aside and let
this thing develop on its own

00:08:08.272 --> 00:08:10.230
and now we're trying to
dig in and we're trying

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:11.722
to be a help to the community.

00:08:11.722 --> 00:08:13.180
I think that you're
starting to see

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.930
a lot more large corporations
take a bet on Node.js,

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:17.100
big companies.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.500
There's a LTS support
model now in place,

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.330
so you know that with
the latest versions,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.621
you're going to get 18 months
of backward compatibility

00:08:23.621 --> 00:08:24.727
and security patches.

00:08:24.727 --> 00:08:27.060
Kind of in my mind, I think
the big thing that's shifted

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:28.870
is it's starting to get real.

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:30.630
I think at first,
it was great if you

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.380
were building small-time
web applications,

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.254
startups, you needed to
do something quickly,

00:08:34.254 --> 00:08:35.309
it was a popular choice.

00:08:35.309 --> 00:08:37.747
But now we can use this to
build real web applications.

00:08:37.747 --> 00:08:39.330
It's actually harder
to find companies

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.080
that aren't using
Node at this stage

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:42.476
if they're doing
web development.

00:08:42.476 --> 00:08:43.850
MARK MANDEL: Cool
so-- all right.

