of course that's going
to work same as it would

00:09:12.108 --> 00:09:13.157
any other cloud provider.

00:09:13.157 --> 00:09:14.990
I think the things that
make running Node.js

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:17.720
in Google Cloud interesting,
unique, and useful

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.420
are both Google
Container Engine, which

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.780
is the hosted
version of Kubernetes

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:24.870
for running Docker
containers at scale,

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:26.300
and then, of course, App Engine.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.300
So I'll start with
App Engine then

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.060
we can talk a little
bit about Kubernetes.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:32.150
With App Engine, it's just sort
of the idea is you show up,

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:34.990
you bring your code,
and we run it for you.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:35.940
We scale it.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:38.739
You push it to us and we sort
of take care of the rest.

00:09:38.739 --> 00:09:40.530
And if you want to dig
in and customize it,

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.404
at the end of the day,
all we're really doing

00:09:42.404 --> 00:09:44.860
is running Docker
containers for you.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:46.800
So if you want to go
customize the image,

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:49.424
install something that you want
on top of the operating system,

00:09:49.424 --> 00:09:51.849
something like ImageMagick,
FFmpeg, that sort of thing,

00:09:51.849 --> 00:09:54.390
you can just sort of take over
and define what the runtime is

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:55.384
going to look like.

00:09:55.384 --> 00:09:57.800
But at the end of the day, the
idea is you bring the code,

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:58.580
we run it.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:01.230
MARK MANDEL: So do we like--
I mean, obviously, we're

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.240
sounding like-- we
said we released

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.840
beta support for Node.js.

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:06.590
Like what does
that actually mean?

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:08.630
You said we can just
take the code and run it.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.919
What does that actually
end up looking like?

00:10:10.919 --> 00:10:13.210
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Sure, so
when you come to the platform

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.840
after you do the sign up,
you use the Cloud SDK.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.340
You can run-- you build
your web application,

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.076
have it inside a directory,
run gcloud app deploy.

00:10:21.076 --> 00:10:22.450
And then after
that, you're going

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.900
to get a website running
on an appspot domain.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.650
So you would
getmyapplication.appspot.com.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.010
And that's going
to scale up to, I

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:33.290
think by default, somewhere
between 2 and 20 instances

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.474
running on our platform.

00:10:35.474 --> 00:10:36.890
But really what
the beta was about

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.324
was just kind of signifying
that all the pieces that come

00:10:39.324 --> 00:10:40.740
to building a
Node.js application,

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.260
it actually goes beyond
what's in App Engine.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:46.250
And I think all those pieces
are falling into place.

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:49.640
So that's-- the Docker image
that we use to run your app,

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:51.980
that's now open source
on GitHub, Node.js,

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:55.300
Docker and we publish
that out to GCRIO.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.660
We have the gcloud
npm module is actually

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:59.500
a really important
piece of this.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.060
So this is the npm module
that provides access

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.630
to all of the APIs
and services-- things

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:08.096
like BigQuery, Bigtable,
Cloud Vision API.

00:11:08.096 --> 00:11:09.970
We're starting to work
on the Speech API, all

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.010
of this cool stuff we got
to announce last week,

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:15.380
or I guess now two
weeks ago, at GCP Next.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:18.050
And finally logging
integration-- what is it

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:19.865
like to emit logs
from the platform

00:11:19.865 --> 00:11:21.860
and have an easy way
to get access to those?

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.610
Error Reporting--
just sort of all

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:25.572
of these things that
sit around the platform

00:11:25.572 --> 00:11:27.280
and aren't necessarily
running your code,

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.363
but the things that are
really important when it's

00:11:29.363 --> 00:11:32.970
time to build your application,
monitor it, diagnose it, all

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.104
those sorts of bits.

00:11:34.104 --> 00:11:35.520
MARK MANDEL: So
really we've got--

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.395
we've got all the tools
now to build whatever

00:11:37.395 --> 00:11:40.044
it is you need to use using
all the pieces of our platform.

00:11:40.044 --> 00:11:42.210
And they may not necessarily
have been there before,

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:43.127
but they're there now.

00:11:43.127 --> 00:11:44.043
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:44.043 --> 00:11:45.870
There's something that
I wanted to mention,

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:49.860
which is so I recently watched
your talk at GCP Next, which

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.190
was really awesome.

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:52.325
Congratulations on that.

00:11:52.325 --> 00:11:53.450
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Thank you.

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:55.158
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
really loved the demo.

00:11:55.158 --> 00:11:58.760
That nuclear hat--
that was really cool.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.940
But one of the things
that you showed

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:07.040
was how to basically
move from App Engine

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:10.157
to Container Engine
in a very easy way.

00:12:10.157 --> 00:12:11.990
Could you talk a little
bit more about that?

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.130
Because I thought it
was very interesting.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:14.370
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Yeah.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.140
I think that's
actually the best part

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:18.240
about App Engine right now.

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.540
We started off as this
ecosystem was a little bit

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:22.280
closed off, a
little bit-- vendor

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:24.350
specific APIs,
that sort of thing.

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:27.132
And it's now reached a point
where really what we're doing

00:12:27.132 --> 00:12:29.090
is creating an abstraction
that makes it easier

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:31.047
to run Docker containers.

00:12:31.047 --> 00:12:32.630
So if you're not
familiar with Docker,

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:33.840
you don't need to know
anything about it.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.230
You just bring in
the code and it runs.

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:36.688
But if you are
familiar with Docker

00:12:36.688 --> 00:12:38.930
and if you already have
a workflow for handling

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:41.080
this kind of work,
then what you can do

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.450
is just run a single
command-- gcloud preview

00:12:43.450 --> 00:12:46.341
app gen-config --custom.

00:12:46.341 --> 00:12:47.840
We can get some
resources, I'm sure,

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:49.940
after this for those who are
interested in in doing it.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:52.560
And it's going to generate the
Docker file that we use inside

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.240
of the runtime when we run
it on top of App Engine.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.730
So after that, if you want
to run on top of Kubernetes,

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:00.340
all you have to do is
stand up your cluster,

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:03.816
run Docker Build to build
your Docker image that

00:13:03.816 --> 00:13:05.440
has your application
code, and then you

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.440
can deploy, just like you
would any other Docker

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.100
container into Kubernetes.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.420
So it's really flexible
up and down the stack.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:12.878
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:13:12.878 --> 00:13:16.400
That also means that you can
run the container directly

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:17.740
in your machine, right?

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:19.160
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:20.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very nice.

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:21.363
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:13:21.363 --> 00:13:23.300
So you can basically
start with Managed VMs

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:25.080
sort of just to get you
going, get you up and running,

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:26.480
and then as you need
more flexibility,

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.320
you need to sort of
run down that computer

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:30.940
is a continuum spectrum we've
sort of talked about before.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:32.940
You've got that option
to just pick up and move.

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:33.919
That's very powerful.

00:13:33.919 --> 00:13:35.960
JUSTIN BECKWITH: I think
where that really shines

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:37.668
is where you want to
extend beyond what's

00:13:37.668 --> 00:13:39.320
in a common Node.js application.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:42.094
So like I mentioned, stuff like
ImageMagick, FFmpeg, or let's

00:13:42.094 --> 00:13:43.510
say you want to
be able to support

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.776
multiple languages inside of
the same image at the same time.

00:13:46.776 --> 00:13:49.150
You can completely take that
thing over, install whatever

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.400
you want on it, and then it
can run either an App Engine

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.150
with the automatic
scaling and management,

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.570
or over on Kubernetes
if you need the control.

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:56.882
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:13:56.882 --> 00:13:58.840
So I think we should
probably talk a little bit

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:01.006
about the elf in the room,
considering we're talking

00:14:01.006 --> 00:14:04.560
about JavaScript, so Parse.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.700
Let's talk about Parse.

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:10.170
How does our strategy
around that sort of fit?

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:11.620
We keep getting it at events.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.411
People will come up to
us and they're like,

00:14:13.411 --> 00:14:15.932
oh so can I run my Parse code
on like Google Cloud Platform

00:14:15.932 --> 00:14:17.140
and what does that look like?

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:18.800
What do I do?

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:20.540
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Yeah, this is one

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:21.350
of those things we get a lot.

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:22.340
It especially makes
it interesting

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:24.130
the fact that we
have Firebase, right,

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:25.902
and Firebase base
is owned by Google

00:14:25.902 --> 00:14:27.110
and runs on Google Cloud now.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:28.760
So when you start
talking about Parse,

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.177
then everything gets a
little bit interesting.

00:14:31.177 --> 00:14:33.260
But yeah, we support--
obviously, we support both.

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:35.850
One of the great things that
Facebook did-- I was actually

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:38.100
really, really impressed by
this-- when they announced

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.990
that they were going to stop
working on Parse as a platform,

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.140
as a service, they
right out of the gate

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:45.390
announced that they were
open sourcing the components,

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:47.690
made everything
available to developers,

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:49.927
and they did that as
a Node.js application.

00:14:49.927 --> 00:14:52.510
So if you want to go run Parse
Server today, if you've already

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:54.387
built an app and you've
deployed it to Parse

00:14:54.387 --> 00:14:56.220
and you're looking for
a way to get that off

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.399
of Facebook as they start
to turn the service down,

00:14:58.399 --> 00:15:00.190
they've made it really
easy to take and run

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:01.830
that on other platforms.

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:03.414
And of course one
of the platforms

00:15:03.414 --> 00:15:04.830
where that's going
to run is going

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:07.080
to be on Google App Engine.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:08.480
So we have some samples.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.040
We have some
documentation out there.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:11.364
We did a blog post.

00:15:11.364 --> 00:15:13.530
Really, getting started
this thing is pretty simple.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.990
You just need to deploy
the Parse Server Node.js

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:18.660
application, stand
up MongoDB instance.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:22.270
We used Mongo labs, I think,
for our sample when we did this.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:23.920
And then it's pretty
easy to migrate.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:24.500
MARK MANDEL: So
that's pretty cool.

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:26.190
I guess that's almost
testament as well

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:28.620
to the work that's
gone into making

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.050
the beta support for Node.js,
that you can take something

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.091
like the Parse open source
framework and be like,

00:15:33.091 --> 00:15:34.054
OK, this isn't so bad.

00:15:34.054 --> 00:15:35.970
We just got to kind of
write the documentation

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:37.270
to make this happen.

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:39.050
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Yeah, it's
kind of a funny story actually.

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:41.350
When we first read the news
that this was happening,

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:44.070
I think it was about
2:00, and I went over

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:46.430
to Jason Dobry, who's one
of the platform engineers

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:47.750
that works on Node.js with us.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.060
He's built a lot of
the documentation,

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:51.550
a lot of the samples, a lot
of the code we have out there

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:52.500
on GitHub.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:54.210
And we just kind
of casually started

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:56.210
talking about, what would
it take to get this up

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:57.210
and running?

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:59.824
And I expected to come back
like later in the evening

00:15:59.824 --> 00:16:00.740
and take a look at it.

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:02.820
An hour later, I got an
email from him saying

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:04.111
that he had the sample already.

00:16:04.111 --> 00:16:06.490
He had a pull request
on GitHub ready to go.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.600
So it was over the course
of an hour, he had that.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.247
An hour later, we
had a blog post.

00:16:10.247 --> 00:16:12.080
An hour after that,
there was documentation.

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:14.100
And the evening that
it was announced,

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:15.520
we had everything
up and running,

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:17.960
so it's really a testament
to how great the Facebook

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:19.950
guys did in making
this stuff easily

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.560
portable on other platform,

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:22.685
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:16:22.685 --> 00:16:26.800
Also I guess it's also testament
to how App Engine is not

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:30.230
vendor-locked, as
people tend to say.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:32.790
You can get any code and
just run it there directly.

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:33.615
That is very cool.

00:16:33.615 --> 00:16:35.740
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Yeah, it's
a big change from where

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:37.990
we were back in 2008, you know?

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:40.770
App Engine has been around for
a long time and a lot of things

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:41.740
have changed.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:43.500
Pretty much any
npm module you want

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:46.426
to use for Node.js, any
web server, anything,

00:16:46.426 --> 00:16:48.550
you just show up and, within
reason, we can run it.

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:49.675
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:16:49.675 --> 00:16:53.317
As part of your talk, if I
remember correctly-- or maybe

00:16:53.317 --> 00:16:55.400
there was a different talk
and I'm mixing things--

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:57.720
but I think that you
had someone else talking

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.039
about Node.js for companies.

00:17:00.039 --> 00:17:01.830
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Yeah,
so that was actually

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:05.390
during the GCP Next talk,
and I had Joe McCann, who's

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:10.160
the CEO of NodeSource, a company
that does enterprise Node.js,

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.214
up on stage talking with us.

00:17:12.214 --> 00:17:14.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There were
some announcements there.

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:16.744
Maybe you could share with us?

00:17:16.744 --> 00:17:17.660
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Yeah.

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:19.530
So the big thing that we
wanted to do announce at Next

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:21.480
was just that we formed a
partnership with NodeSource.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.160
Source And what that
means is that we're

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:26.150
able to take the enterprise
distribution of Node.js

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:28.170
that they've built and
that they support, along

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:30.010
with all the tooling
in their ecosystem,

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:32.670
and easily run that on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:34.650
So today, really
what that means is

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:37.880
it's a separate
distribution of Node that

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:40.020
comes with performance
analysis tools that

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:43.650
make it easy to debug things
like memory leaks, high CPU

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:46.105
utilization, produces
flame graphs,

00:17:46.105 --> 00:17:48.631
a variety of great
visualizations to help dig in

00:17:48.631 --> 00:17:50.130
and really, really
understand what's

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:53.510
happening with Node.js
processes in the event

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:55.929
they start to run amok,
as on occasion they do.

00:17:55.929 --> 00:17:57.470
MARK MANDEL: OK so
I guess that means

00:17:57.470 --> 00:17:59.980
we've got some stuff there that
people can just kind of get up

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:01.360
with and just run
with from NodeSource

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:02.590
that they're able to
run on our platform

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:04.090
just like they would normally?

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:05.130
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:06.504
Right now most of
the offering is

00:18:06.504 --> 00:18:09.030
centered around Compute Engine,
but we're working really

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:10.990
hard to bring that to the
other layers of the stack,

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:13.281
down through Container Engine
and App Engine eventually

00:18:13.281 --> 00:18:13.896
as well.

00:18:13.896 --> 00:18:14.770
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.353
So I'm actually quite
curious as well.

00:18:16.353 --> 00:18:17.810
Obviously, you've
some experience

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:20.046
working with Node developers
here on our platform.

00:18:20.046 --> 00:18:21.420
What's the coolest
project you've

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:25.170
seen of people building stuff
with Node on anything on Google

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:26.190
Cloud Platform?

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:27.980
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Wow, anything
on the Google Cloud Platform

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:28.550
so far?

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:29.508
MARK MANDEL: With Node.

00:18:29.508 --> 00:18:31.856
With Node.

00:18:31.856 --> 00:18:32.980
Just to narrow it slightly.

00:18:32.980 --> 00:18:36.746
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Other than
making a nuclear cap turn.

00:18:36.746 --> 00:18:38.370
See, I thought that
was pretty much it,

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:39.660
connecting it to little bits.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:40.560
You know, it has
everything, right?

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:41.250
It's got IoT.

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:42.630
It's got WebSockets.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:44.820
It's got Node.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.351
It was end to end
on the platform.

00:18:46.351 --> 00:18:48.850
MARK MANDEL: Well so actually
we'll back track for a second.

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:50.400
We've talked a little
about your presentation.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:51.780
For those people who
haven't seen this,

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:53.310
do you want to talk
us through the demo

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:54.893
you had and then
maybe we'll come back

00:18:54.893 --> 00:18:56.450
to our original question?

00:18:56.450 --> 00:18:59.090
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Sure,
so it was just a few

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.630
demos-- some serious, some
just for fun, quite honestly,

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:04.970
because they amused me-- but all
with the idea of showing of how

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:06.700
to use Node.js on our platform.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:10.010
So the first one we did was
with the Cloud Vision API.

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:11.720
We did a little
analysis of Reddit.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:12.720
Actually, went on r/aww.

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:15.322
We were detecting
images on Reddit

00:19:15.322 --> 00:19:17.530
to figure out if they were
dogs or cats, putting them

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:19.980
in the right category, and
kind of doing real time data

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:21.834
analytics on stage.

00:19:21.834 --> 00:19:22.750
That one that was fun.

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:23.890
I was really,
really worried when

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:25.880
I was doing that demo that
Reddit was going to go down

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:27.588
and I was going to be
left standing there

00:19:27.588 --> 00:19:30.575
with an empty webpage,
but it all worked out.

00:19:30.575 --> 00:19:32.700
And then the last demo that
we started put together

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.660
was on using littleBits.

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:37.820
littleBits are small, composable
electronic building blocks,

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:42.190
usually for kids or
for the young at heart,

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:44.180
and they have this thing
called a cloudBit that

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:45.500
let's you connect
them to the internet.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.100
So we started playing
around with those

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.475
and seeing what it would be
like to connect it to Node.js

00:19:49.475 --> 00:19:50.780
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:53.127
And I think during that, there
was too much interference

00:19:53.127 --> 00:19:54.960
in the room and not
everything tied together

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.935
the way I would have liked,
but it was still pretty cool.

00:19:57.935 --> 00:19:58.810
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:01.440
And the final result
was a twirling propeller

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:02.274
on your nuclear hat?

00:20:02.274 --> 00:20:03.314
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Oh yeah.

00:20:03.314 --> 00:20:04.000
It played music.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:04.810
It twirled the hat.

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:05.740
It did everything.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:11.860
It was-- lots of engineering
manpower going into that hat.

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:14.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Definitely
worth the effort.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:14.760
MARK MANDEL: That's great.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:15.280
OK, cool.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:19.270
So there's a bunch of work
going into Node.js Google Cloud

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:19.930
Platform.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:23.310
Are you able to hint at
anything, like what's upcoming,

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.320
what things should
people expect, you know,

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:26.374
that sort of good stuff?

00:20:26.374 --> 00:20:27.790
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:30.680
So phase one for us was kind of
going and making all this stuff

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.760
run great on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:36.020
So we talked about Trace
and Debug and App Engine

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:38.410
and the integration with
NodeSource and all the fun

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:39.610
stuff around that.

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:42.280
I think the next step is
actually integrating ourselves

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:45.460
more closely with Node.js
as an organization

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:47.690
and as an underlying technology.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:49.452
So we've built up
all these years,

00:20:49.452 --> 00:20:51.660
as I mentioned, years of
muscle and making JavaScript

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:52.910
work great on the web browser.

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:55.159
Well the next step is to
actually dig in and help make

00:20:55.159 --> 00:20:56.326
it run better on the server.

00:20:56.326 --> 00:20:57.867
I don't think that
we've done as much

00:20:57.867 --> 00:21:00.380
there as we've always wanted
to in the early days of Node.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.790
And I think we could have
a huge impact doing things

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:04.940
like making the developer
tooling in Chrome

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:07.040
work better with Node.js,
make the debugging

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:09.340
story a little bit
easier, make the tooling

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:12.530
story a little bit better,
improve the performance.

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:15.155
One of the things that we've
done to actually kick off

00:21:15.155 --> 00:21:18.090
this next stage of work, now
that we're past the beta,

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:20.795
is we've actually just recently
joined the Node.js foundation,

00:21:20.795 --> 00:21:23.170
and that's a group that's
responsible for driving Node.js

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:25.960
as a technology forward--
not just on Google Cloud,

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:29.275
but making Node better
for developers everywhere.

00:21:29.275 --> 00:21:30.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:33.630
That actually shows
a lot of commitment

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:38.560
from Google, being
part of it, really.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:44.480
But what else could people
working with Node.js

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:47.320
expect to see soon on
Google Cloud Platform?

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:50.830
We have a bunch of APIs and
they can run on App Engine.

00:21:50.830 --> 00:21:52.039
It's beta.

00:21:52.039 --> 00:21:53.580
Could tell us a
little bit more about

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:56.730
is there any specific
roadmap that you could share?

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:59.074
You're a product manager, so
maybe you have that power.

00:21:59.074 --> 00:22:00.490
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:01.590
I think the biggest
things you're

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.048
going to see from
us moving forward

00:22:03.048 --> 00:22:05.650
are better API support
for all the cool services.

00:22:05.650 --> 00:22:07.220
So we announced a
lot of great stuff

00:22:07.220 --> 00:22:11.000
at GCP Next, things like Cloud
ML, the Google Cloud Speech

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:11.877
API.

00:22:11.877 --> 00:22:14.210
As we start to turn out more
and more of these services,

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:16.190
then we have to
expose meaningful APIs

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:18.752
for Node developers-- not
just on Google Cloud Platform,

00:22:18.752 --> 00:22:20.710
but if you want to take
advantage of these APIs

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:23.762
from your own data centers, from
other clouds, we don't care.

00:22:23.762 --> 00:22:25.970
However you want to do it,
we want to kind of open up

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:29.340
these sort of, I like to call
them the Google Cloud magic

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:31.814
APIs, all the stuff that
does machine learning.

00:22:31.814 --> 00:22:33.480
We're going to be
spending a lot of time

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.180
there, improving those
APIs and getting them

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:36.150
in front of everyone.

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:37.274
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:22:37.274 --> 00:22:39.860
We're pretty much out
of time, but is there

00:22:39.860 --> 00:22:42.420
anything else that you
would like to mention

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:44.180
that we didn't have time to?

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:45.680
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Yeah, the big thing

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:48.010
is just we love
JavaScript here at Google.

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:48.780
We love the web.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:50.488
We like connecting
things to the internet

00:22:50.488 --> 00:22:51.850
and we like running services.

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:54.150
And we're committed to
making Google Cloud the best

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:55.820
place to run
Node.js applications

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:58.980
and we're committed to
making Node.js itself better.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:01.800
So if you're looking for a place
to bring your code, come to us.

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:02.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is awesome.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:03.490
MARK MANDEL: Well
thank you so much,

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:05.073
Justin, for spending
the time with us.

00:23:05.073 --> 00:23:06.180
We really appreciate it.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:06.470
JUSTIN BECKWITH: All right.

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:07.261
Thanks a lot, guys.

00:23:07.261 --> 00:23:07.980
This was fun.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:09.265
MARK MANDEL: Thanks a lot.

00:23:09.265 --> 00:23:11.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool,
thanks again to Justin

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:14.790
for such an awesome
conversation-- learned a lot,

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:16.210
had lots of fun.

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:18.210
We will have a bunch
of links in the show

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:21.630
notes to all the little
resources that he mentioned,

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:24.180
the talk where you can see
him wearing a nuclear hat that

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:27.890
turns around, so lots of fun.

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:29.641
Before we finish
for today, let's

00:23:29.641 --> 00:23:31.140
talk about the
Question of the Week.

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:35.102
We said it was a Bigtable,
but why don't you, Mark,

00:23:35.102 --> 00:23:37.060
tell us a little bit
about the question itself?

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:37.470
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:39.250
So we got a nice
email from a gentleman

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.280
by the name of Michael McKenzie.

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:42.950
His question is,
I think Bigtable

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:44.860
could be used in one of
my personal projects,

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:46.901
but I do not know
where to start.

00:23:46.901 --> 00:23:48.650
He's actually read the
Google paper on it.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:49.580
Good for him.

00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:51.350
That's a job well done.

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:53.750
However, I find myself a bit
lost in fully understanding

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:54.510
how to get going.

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:56.260
That said, do you have
any recommendations

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:57.880
of simple apps or
projects one can

00:23:57.880 --> 00:23:59.584
get acquainted with Bigtable?

00:23:59.584 --> 00:24:00.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:02.280
MARK MANDEL: So I feel like this
is kind of like a two-parter,

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:02.980
actually.

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:04.813
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think
that the first part

00:24:04.813 --> 00:24:08.430
is when you say you're
planning on using it

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:12.030
for personal
projects, I could say,

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:16.090
think about it twice, unless
your personal projects have

00:24:16.090 --> 00:24:18.820
an amazing traffic
and you really

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:25.720
need the throughput
that Bigtable can offer.

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:28.247
I could say that maybe
Datastore is the place to be.

00:24:28.247 --> 00:24:29.830
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
I'd probably almost

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:32.600
look at it as well from
like personal projects.

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:34.300
Bigtable can be expensive.

00:24:34.300 --> 00:24:35.800
We turn around and
say, yeah, if you

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:39.555
want a rather large amount
of data for that cost savings

00:24:39.555 --> 00:24:40.600
to kind of kick in.

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:42.683
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If your
personal project requires

00:24:42.683 --> 00:24:45.640
something like 1 million
operations per second, yes,

00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:46.840
go for it.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:48.790
But if you're around
one per second,

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:52.070
probably Bigtable is
a little bit overkill.

00:24:52.070 --> 00:24:53.350
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, probably.

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:54.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
But if you actually

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:56.540
want to learn more
about Bigtable,

00:24:56.540 --> 00:24:59.900
I think that reading the paper
is a very, very good step--

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:03.196
maybe overkill too.

00:25:03.196 --> 00:25:05.980
It's not probably the
best place to start

00:25:05.980 --> 00:25:08.824
if you want to understand
more, but there's actually

00:25:08.824 --> 00:25:09.740
all the documentation.

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:14.580
We also have a bunch of
sample projects on GitHub.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:16.496
So that's on github.com/googl
ecloudplatform/c

00:25:16.496 --> 00:25:23.650
loud-bigtable-samples, I think.

00:25:23.650 --> 00:25:28.280
But we'll have the links to
those projects on the show

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:29.047
notes.

00:25:29.047 --> 00:25:31.610
And in one of
those examples, you

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:33.610
would actually be able
to see that there's

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:35.988
a tool called Quickstart.

00:25:35.988 --> 00:25:40.490
And Quickstart is
basically a Java tool

00:25:40.490 --> 00:25:44.120
that allows you to use
Bigtable from the command line.

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:45.640
So you can create tables.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:47.190
You can insert data.

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:48.170
You can read from it.

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:50.280
So you can basically
get a little bit

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:53.737
of an idea of how Bigtable
actually works and then you

00:25:53.737 --> 00:25:54.820
can start playing with it.

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.903
Also, it's cool because
if you have a lot of data,

00:25:56.903 --> 00:26:00.550
you can also see how
incredibly fast it works.

00:26:00.550 --> 00:26:03.590
But if you're not a
Java guy, like me,

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:08.180
and you think that Maven is
very hard to understand-- which,

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:09.660
if you ask me, it is.

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:10.870
Also, XML?

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:11.370
Come on.

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:16.280
But there's actually
another tool

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:18.520
that I ended up
using personally,

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:20.130
which is called CBT.

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:23.420
That stands for Cloud
Bigtable, and it's read and go.

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:26.540
So basically you can
just install it directly

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:31.160
or I think that
there's distribution

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:35.639
binaries for Windows and
Mac and Linux and whatever.

00:26:35.639 --> 00:26:38.180
And then you just run it because
you don't need anything else

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:40.480
because it's just a
self-maintained binary,

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:41.560
so that's cool.

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:42.990
MARK MANDEL: Not that
you're biased in any way,

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:43.620
shape, or form.

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:44.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Absolutely not.

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:45.220
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:26:45.220 --> 00:26:45.970
Absolutely not.

00:26:45.970 --> 00:26:46.600
OK cool.

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:48.809
So yeah, if you want to
get started with Bigtable,

00:26:48.809 --> 00:26:50.850
there's no reason why you
couldn't fire up a node

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:52.480
and sort of run it
within the trial period

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:54.410
for like a short period
or something like that.

00:26:54.410 --> 00:26:55.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Three nodes.

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:56.160
MARK MANDEL: Three nodes, yeah.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:56.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is the minimum.

00:26:56.940 --> 00:26:58.300
MARK MANDEL: That is the
minimum, three nodes.

00:26:58.300 --> 00:26:59.250
That's totally true.

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:02.010
But definitely shut that down
again before you're finished.

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:02.926
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:02.926 --> 00:27:03.520
Yeah.

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:06.290
I mean I think it's
a very nice way

00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:09.410
to understand the power of the
Cloud, but at the same time,

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:11.210
if you try for a
personal project,

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:13.650
probably Datastore is
a good place to be.

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:15.150
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
I guess if you're

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:16.320
going to be running
that long term,

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:17.480
Datastore makes a lot of sense.

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:19.835
If you're going to do like a
few little bits and pieces,

00:27:19.835 --> 00:27:21.209
you could run it
for a little bit

00:27:21.209 --> 00:27:23.054
and then turn it back off again.

00:27:23.054 --> 00:27:24.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:26.320
Well thank you so much, Mark.

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:29.302
Before we finish, what are
your plans for next week?

00:27:29.302 --> 00:27:30.260
MARK MANDEL: Next week?

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:31.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Or next weeks?

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:32.260
MARK MANDEL: Next weeks?

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:32.860
I don't know.

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:34.210
I actually think I'm just
going to be hanging out

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:36.660
in San Francisco writing some
content the next few weeks.

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:37.785
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

